From 27adae538ab45b15523c809fcccba548c02fbc94 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:32:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ask the trace for the face the item acts on

A click that acts on one face of a block was asked its question in two spellings: a placement named the side it goes against (the builder spelled the pair out by hand, once for the candidate it was considering at BuilderProcess:385 and once for the click at :603), and everything else asked only for the block. The farmer's two plantings are the second kind and are the ones it matters for: a seed goes into the top of a farmland and a cocoa bean onto the side of a log, so a click sent while the crosshair is on the side of the right block sends the item into a face that does not take it - a packet, an arm swung at nothing, and the tick spent standing there for it, with the process trying again next tick.

The question has a name now, RayTraceUtils#hitsFace, with IPlayerContext#isLookingAt(block, face) for the trace the crosshair is really on and RayTraceUtils#hitsFaceTowards for a caller that knows where the item is going rather than which way the face is called. Five sites adopt it: the farmer's two plantings and the two traces it takes before aiming at them, the builder's candidate and its click, and the movement layer's own placement search, which had the same shape by hand. The farmer's harvest and bone meal keep the block question with the reason written down - a block is broken from whichever side the crosshair is on, and bone meal acts on the block it is used on - so the difference is on the page. Every farmer click also gains the arrival fallback the mining and builder clicks have (isLookingAt or hasArrivedAt of the point the hand was sent to).

The bridge's step-back (MovementTraverse, the sneak-place over air) is the sixth: the block it places goes into dest.below(), which is the position the face of goalLook the bot looks back at points at, so asking for the block alone let a click go out on another side of it and put a block behind the bot, on the bridge it had just left; it asks hitsFaceTowards(goalLook, dest.below()) now.

Validation: the standalone source-scanning run of ClickGatesTest is OK (3 tests), and the full suite is 37 classes / 306 tests / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped in 33s. ClickGatesTest's new pass pins who asks a face - the six sites above, per file and per shape of the call - and it has teeth: putting the farmland planting back to the block question alone fails it with both maps printed, then the file was restored (checked by hand).
